WEBVTT

00:00:00.153 --> 00:00:03.687
Welcome to Module 11, Advanced SQL: CTEs and Window Functions.

00:00:03.687 --> 00:00:06.712
The word advanced can make these topics sound harder than they are.

00:00:06.712 --> 00:00:10.542
Most of the difficulty comes from two questions.

00:00:10.542 --> 00:00:12.842
First, what does each intermediate result represent?

00:00:12.842 --> 00:00:15.892
Second, which related rows are visible to the current calculation?

00:00:15.892 --> 00:00:22.667
We will answer those questions gradually with Common Table Expressions, recursive CTEs, window functions,

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:25.697
ranking, LAG and LEAD, and explicit window frames.

00:00:25.697 --> 00:00:30.802
Every SQL example can run directly in the browser, and you can also copy it into pgAdmin.

00:00:30.802 --> 00:00:33.777
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:33.777 --> 00:00:38.432
a hospital ranking visits and identifying readmissions.

00:00:38.432 --> 00:00:43.867
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:44.402
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:44.402 --> 00:00:51.277
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to defend later:

00:00:51.277 --> 00:00:54.952
what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:00:54.952 --> 00:00:57.440
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
